1
00:00:00,792 --> 00:00:03,875
This is a project film by
Ethan Gomes, about

2
00:00:03,875 --> 00:00:06,000
DataSentry, in four parts.

3
00:00:06,125 --> 00:00:08,167
Part one, the executive summary.

4
00:00:08,583 --> 00:00:11,375
Companies set rules to catch
bad data, like a

5
00:00:11,375 --> 00:00:13,208
taxi fare below zero.

6
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:17,333
DataSentry tests those rules
like a spell-checker:

7
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:20,542
hide mistakes on purpose, count
which get caught, and

8
00:00:20,542 --> 00:00:21,542
which slip through.

9
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:25,125
DataSentry is an AI agent that
writes data checks

10
00:00:25,125 --> 00:00:28,417
for a table, tests them against bad rows I

11
00:00:28,417 --> 00:00:32,458
planted, and revises them, until
a person approves the

12
00:00:32,458 --> 00:00:33,375
final set.

13
00:00:33,417 --> 00:00:36,750
Why it matters: when checks
let bad rows through,

14
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,542
wrong numbers reach
dashboards and decisions.

15
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,125
And as AI writes more
checks, someone has to

16
00:00:43,125 --> 00:00:43,875
grade them.

17
00:00:43,875 --> 00:00:45,541
Part two, what others have done.

18
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:50,666
Pandera, dbt and Great
Expectations run rules on tables;

19
00:00:51,208 --> 00:00:53,125
Amazon's Deequ suggests them.

20
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:56,375
I ran one rule, fare can't be negative, in

21
00:00:56,375 --> 00:01:00,000
all three: all caught the
negative fares, none caught

22
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,083
the trips that end before they start.

23
00:01:02,208 --> 00:01:04,166
AI already writes tests:

24
00:01:04,499 --> 00:01:08,708
Great Expectations' paid ExpectAI
suggests data checks, and

25
00:01:08,708 --> 00:01:12,708
at Meta, AI-written tests built
seventy-five percent of the

26
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:15,124
time and passed fifty-seven;

27
00:01:15,124 --> 00:01:18,958
a later tool, steered by planted
bugs, had seventy-three

28
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:20,083
percent accepted.

29
00:01:20,166 --> 00:01:23,666
Mutation testing has graded code
tests with planted bugs

30
00:01:23,666 --> 00:01:28,249
since 1978. But in what I
found, nobody does

31
00:01:28,249 --> 00:01:31,624
that for AI-written data
checks on real tables.

32
00:01:32,041 --> 00:01:34,124
That's the gap DataSentry fills.

33
00:01:34,207 --> 00:01:37,249
Part three, methods. The data is public:

34
00:01:37,624 --> 00:01:41,124
New York City yellow taxi trips
from January twenty

35
00:01:41,124 --> 00:01:42,957
twenty-five, ten thousand rows.

36
00:01:43,249 --> 00:01:46,332
Step one: a planter script
breaks copies of the

37
00:01:46,332 --> 00:01:49,499
table five different ways, and
locks the answer key

38
00:01:49,499 --> 00:01:49,999
away.

39
00:01:50,124 --> 00:01:53,249
Step two: Claude sees only
the column names and

40
00:01:53,249 --> 00:01:56,457
types, and writes pandera
checks under a contract.

41
00:01:56,999 --> 00:01:59,707
A scorer grades them on the clean copy and

42
00:01:59,707 --> 00:02:01,124
every broken copy.

43
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:02,582
Step three: the loop.

44
00:02:03,124 --> 00:02:06,041
Only the clean copy's results
go back, and Claude

45
00:02:06,041 --> 00:02:08,707
revises, up to three rounds, all logged.

46
00:02:08,957 --> 00:02:10,416
It's built, and it ran.

47
00:02:10,582 --> 00:02:12,041
Its tools are fenced:

48
00:02:12,291 --> 00:02:15,874
no built-in tools, an empty
folder, no answer keys

49
00:02:15,874 --> 00:02:19,082
or held-out data, and every
checks file must pass

50
00:02:19,082 --> 00:02:21,124
a code policy before it runs.

51
00:02:21,624 --> 00:02:24,874
Next: a tool menu, with
every call checked.

52
00:02:24,874 --> 00:02:26,916
And a person stays in charge.

53
00:02:27,166 --> 00:02:30,124
Before any checks are kept,
I'll see the scorecard,

54
00:02:30,249 --> 00:02:33,374
the code and its fingerprint,
and approve, refuse, or

55
00:02:33,374 --> 00:02:36,124
defer. Nothing is kept without that.

56
00:02:36,166 --> 00:02:37,332
Do I have what I need?

57
00:02:37,624 --> 00:02:41,374
Yes: public data, my laptop,
and my existing Claude

58
00:02:41,374 --> 00:02:44,666
subscription. No API key, no credits.

59
00:02:44,874 --> 00:02:49,374
Part four, evaluation. I measure
defect types caught, false

60
00:02:49,374 --> 00:02:53,082
alarms on good trips, and
crashing checks, on held-out

61
00:02:53,082 --> 00:02:54,874
data never used for tuning:

62
00:02:55,290 --> 00:02:57,957
a second planting and a second clean copy.

63
00:02:57,999 --> 00:03:01,124
It passes only with all five
defect types caught,

64
00:03:01,582 --> 00:03:04,832
zero good trips flagged, and no
crashing checks, on

65
00:03:04,832 --> 00:03:07,582
both plantings. Anything less fails.

66
00:03:07,832 --> 00:03:09,582
By hand, nothing passed.

67
00:03:09,957 --> 00:03:13,165
In the loop, round one flagged
ninety-four good trips,

68
00:03:13,373 --> 00:03:15,832
round two just one, and
round three none, with

69
00:03:15,832 --> 00:03:18,373
all five caught: pass line met.

70
00:03:18,832 --> 00:03:21,165
On the second clean copy, still zero.

71
00:03:21,373 --> 00:03:24,165
I'll also break it on
purpose, at least five

72
00:03:24,165 --> 00:03:27,082
ways, like a forbidden import or a try at

73
00:03:27,082 --> 00:03:30,123
the answer key. And one
controlled failure:

74
00:03:30,498 --> 00:03:33,665
I'll trigger the rule-dropping
Claude did by hand;

75
00:03:33,957 --> 00:03:36,915
the held-out scorer must catch
it, and I'll refuse

76
00:03:36,915 --> 00:03:37,957
it at the gate.

77
00:03:37,998 --> 00:03:40,248
So far, it worked on this table and five

78
00:03:40,248 --> 00:03:42,748
defect types. Not shown yet:

79
00:03:42,998 --> 00:03:45,790
other tables, or real
defects I didn't plant.

80
00:03:46,207 --> 00:03:48,707
That's DataSentry. I'm Ethan Gomes.

81
00:03:48,873 --> 00:03:49,832
Thanks for watching.
